    correctAnswer: "Reliability",
    notes: `<b>RELIABILITY</b><br><br><b>1. Definition</b><br><br>Reliability refers to the consistency of a psychological test's results. A reliable test produces consistent scores when the conditions are similar.<br><br><b>2. Explanation</b><br><br>Reliability tells us how consistent a test is and how much measurement error may affect its scores.<br><br>A test is more reliable when its results are less affected by random errors.<br><br>There are two general types to remember:<br><ul><li><b>Internal Reliability</b> – Consistency within the test itself. Items that measure the same thing should give consistent results.</li><li><b>External Consistency</b> – Consistency when results are compared across different testing occasions, test forms, or raters.</li></ul><b>Common Methods of Estimating Reliability</b><br><ul><li><b>Test-Retest Reliability</b> – Gives the same test to the same people on two different occasions and compares their scores.</li><li><b>Parallel-Forms Reliability</b> – Compares scores from two equivalent forms of a test.</li><li><b>Internal Consistency</b> – Examines how consistently the items in a test measure a characteristic.</li><li><b>Inter-Rater Reliability</b> – Examines how consistently different raters or scorers evaluate the same person or performance.</li></ul><b>3. Example</b><br><br>A student takes a reliable ability test twice under similar conditions. The student's scores are similar on both occasions.<br><br>This suggests that the test has good test-retest reliability.<br><br><b>4. What to Remember for CFE</b><br><ul><li>Reliability = consistency of scores.</li><li>Higher reliability means less measurement error.</li><li>Test-retest reliability focuses on consistency over time.</li><li>Parallel-forms reliability focuses on consistency between equivalent test forms.</li><li>Internal consistency focuses on consistency among test items.</li><li>Inter-rater reliability focuses on agreement between raters or scorers.</li></ul><b>5. Situational Application</b><br><br><b>Situation:</b> Two psychologists independently score the same person's assessment, and their ratings are very similar.<br><br><b>Answer:</b> Inter-Rater Reliability.<br><br><b>Why?</b> It measures the consistency of scores given by different raters.<br><br><b>6. CFE Tip: How to Recognize It</b><br><br><b>Clue words:</b> consistency, stability, similar scores, measurement error, agreement between raters.<br><br><b>Easy way to remember:</b><br><br><b>Reliability = Consistency.</b><br><br>A reliable test gives consistent results, while an unreliable test may produce results that vary because of measurement error.`
